  "why": "The paper empirically demonstrates that a robustness/repair mechanism analogous to adversarial training can be circumvented by transformed inputs, although safeguard bypass is not the fuzzer's explicit design objective. Its standard ETI-based fine-tuning is explicitly described as being adapted from adversarial/robust training and is intended to repair mispredictions. After applying this fine-tuning, the authors test the resulting models and find that they become less resilient to some transformations: the random attack success rate (RASR) rises, in some cases to 98.5% or 100%. Thus, transformed inputs successfully induce failures despite the model having been fine-tuned for robustness, constituting an empirical safeguard/robustness bypass. However, the EP-oriented oracle and exploration policy are designed to comprehensively discover transformation-specific erroneous properties and to avoid local testing plateaus, not to target a defender, detector, or adversarially trained safeguard based on safeguard feedback. Therefore this is a demonstrated bypass without a bypass-specific fuzzer design, which matches Medium rather than High.",
